          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01E71X2DMoRi84cSKusph4ao",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/log-metric-correlation.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Log/Metric Correlation\\ndescription: Propagating trace context (trace_id, span_id) into log records and metric exemplars to enable pivoting from observed metric anomalies to causative traces.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Observability\\n  - concept/OpenTelemetry\\n  - pattern/Correlation\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single authoritative source (OpenTelemetry definition); mechanics described but not yet cross-verified.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nLog/metric correlation is an observability technique that links metric anomalies to their causative traces by embedding trace context into log records and metric exemplars.\\n\\n## Mechanics\\n\\nThe pattern works by propagating a request\\u0027s **trace ID** and **span ID** into two artifacts:\\n\\n1. **Log records**: Each structured log event carries the \\u0060trace_id\\u0060 and \\u0060span_id\\u0060 from its originating span, allowing logs produced during a traced request to be queried and filtered by that trace.\\n\\n2. **Metric exemplars**: Exemplars are specific measurements associated with a metric point (counter increment, histogram sample, gauge reading) that include trace context. When a metric spike occurs, the exemplar attached to that spike carries the \\u0060trace_id\\u0060 and \\u0060span_id\\u0060 that caused the measurement.\\n\\n## Operator Workflow\\n\\nWith trace context embedded in both signals:\\n\\n1. An operator observes an anomaly on a metric dashboard (e.g., a spike in request latency or error rate).\\n2. The operator retrieves the exemplar associated with that metric point, extracting its \\u0060trace_id\\u0060.\\n3. Using that \\u0060trace_id\\u0060, the operator queries the trace backend to retrieve the full causative trace, which shows the sequence of operations and where latency accumulated.\\n4. The same \\u0060trace_id\\u0060 can be used to retrieve related log records from that time window, providing additional context about what the system was doing.\\n\\nThis pivot from signal (metric) \\u2192 exemplar (trace context) \\u2192 trace (spans) \\u2192 logs (detailed events) provides a structured, tool-agnostic path to root cause.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: OpenTelemetry Specification. Three signal types: traces (causally-linked spans), metrics (aggregatable numeric measurements), and logs (timestamped structured events).\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
